<Base title="When should you add another data system?" description="Investigate search and analytical work inside a relational database, then reason through the freshness, recovery and operating responsibilities of another copy.">
 <article class="visual-essay relational-design">
  <header class="essay-header"><p class="eyebrow">Databases and storage</p><h1>When should you add another data system?</h1><TitleLine type="Relational deep dive"><a href="/learn/databases/relational/">Relational introduction ↗</a></TitleLine></header>
<ArticleBody>
  <p>A shop has a relational database containing its products, prices and orders. Now its search box needs to understand words, and its weekly report has begun competing with checkout for resources. Adding a search service or analytical store may help. It also creates another place where the shop’s facts must arrive, remain intelligible and be recovered when something goes wrong.</p>
  <p>The decision starts by separating the work the application needs from the name of the system that might perform it. Searching product text, ranking matches, joining order lines and scanning a year of sales are different operations. Some can be served well inside the existing engine. Others may justify a specialised system once their requirements and costs are visible.</p>
  <p>We will follow product search from a query inside PostgreSQL to a separately maintained copy. The same questions recur when moving reporting or other derived work: which system owns the fact, how current the answer must be, and what happens while the copy is incomplete. The PostgreSQL examples use version 18; the browser lessons run its PGlite implementation in one connection.</p>
  <section id="requirement"><h2>Describe the missing behaviour</h2>
   <p>“We need search” leaves too much unspecified. A customer typing <code>blue mug</code> may expect plural forms to match, misspellings to be tolerated, in-stock products to rank higher and filters to apply consistently. A support agent searching an exact order reference needs a different operation. Neither request establishes that the current database lacks the necessary access path.</p>
   <p>Collect representative queries and the results people consider useful. Include an uncommon product, an ambiguous phrase, a typo, a newly added item and a withdrawn item. Decide whether a missing recent item is tolerable and whether showing a withdrawn one is merely confusing or violates a concrete requirement. Those answers determine both the search test and the copy’s freshness obligations.</p>
   <p>For a report, specify its result just as carefully. Is it a live total used to approve a purchase, an hourly operational summary or a closed monthly account? A delayed summary can be acceptable for one and wrong for another. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/reporting/">reporting article</a> follows this distinction through consistent reads and materialised results. Moving a query does not remove the need to define what time its answer describes.</p>
   <p>Then measure the current problem. A slow request might spend most of its time waiting for a connection or making repeated round trips. Another might scan more columns and rows than its result needs. Fixing those patterns can change the capacity question before a second system enters it. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/request-cost/">request-cost guide</a> and <a href="/learn/databases/relational/slow-queries/">query investigation</a> show how to identify that work.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="native-search"><h2>Full-text search in PostgreSQL</h2>
   <p>PostgreSQL can turn text into searchable terms and match a query against them. Its <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/textsearch.html">full-text search</a> facilities include language configurations, ranking functions and specialised indexes. These are capabilities inside the relational engine; storing records in tables does not limit every lookup to equality on a scalar column.</p>
   <p>The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=search-in-postgres">search lesson</a> creates three products: Blue mugs, Red mug and Blue bowl. A stored generated column applies <code>to_tsvector('english', title)</code> to each title. For the first product, the English configuration produces the terms <code>blue</code> and <code>mug</code>, reducing the plural. A GIN index stores an access path over those terms. The query uses the same configuration:</p>
   <Sql code={search} label="Match the normalised words inside PostgreSQL" />
   <p><code>plainto_tsquery</code> combines the surviving input terms with AND, and <code>@@</code> tests the match. Only Blue mugs contains both required terms. This is different from looking for one exact substring, and also different from a promise to understand every spelling or intention. The lesson’s misspelled query demonstrates that language normalisation does not automatically provide typo correction.</p>
   <p>Now rename that product to Blue ceramic cup. PostgreSQL maintains the stored generated value and its index as part of changing the row. After the transaction commits, a new Read Committed query sees the new terms: <code>blue mug</code> no longer matches it, while <code>blue cup</code> does. There is no separate application copying job between this row and its generated search representation. The <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/textsearch-tables.html">documented generated-column arrangement</a> makes that relationship explicit.</p>
   <p>Three products establish the matching behaviour, not its capacity. Load representative titles, languages and term frequencies before measuring. Common words can select many products; filters and ranking add work; changing indexed text adds maintenance. Inspect the actual plan, including how candidates are filtered and sorted. PostgreSQL's <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/textsearch-indexes.html">text-search index guide</a> describes the supported access paths, but the index alone cannot tell you whether the resulting ranking suits your customers. The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/full-text-search/">full-text chapter</a> follows positions, phrases, ranking and filtered retrieval through a larger worked example.</p>
   <p>The useful test has two parts: does the answer meet the product’s relevance and freshness requirements, and can the existing service perform that work alongside its writes? If the answer is yes, keeping the search there avoids a separate copy. If relevance needs or sustained resource competition remain unsatisfied, those observations give a specialised candidate something concrete to improve.</p>
   <p>Apply the same reasoning to other capabilities. A better query, summary table, materialised view or suitable index might solve a reporting problem. An extension can add functionality, subject to version, support and hosting constraints. A separate analytical engine can still be the right choice for broad scans or distinct operating needs; the <a href="/learn/databases/column-stores/">column-store essay</a> explains why a different physical layout can change that work. Evaluate the mechanism and workload together.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="another-copy"><h2>What another copy changes</h2>
   <p>Suppose the shop chooses a separate search service. The catalogue database remains authoritative for product identity and whether an item may be sold. Search holds a derived document containing the title and selected attributes. A customer’s search request asks the copy for candidates; checkout must still enforce the purchase rules against authoritative state.</p>
   <div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Product withdrawal reaching a search copy"><table><thead><tr><th>Event</th><th>Catalogue</th><th>Search copy</th><th>Consequence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Initial state</td><td>Product 1, version 1, active</td><td>Version 1, active</td><td>Search offers the product</td></tr><tr><td>Withdrawal commits</td><td>Version 2, inactive</td><td>Version 1, active</td><td>A stale search can still offer it</td></tr><tr><td>Withdrawal is applied</td><td>Version 2, inactive</td><td>Version 2, inactive</td><td>Search stops offering it</td></tr><tr><td>An old update is replayed</td><td>Version 2, inactive</td><td>Must retain version 2</td><td>The replay must not restore the old offer</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
   <p>The interval between the second and third rows exists even when every component is working. A failed updater can make it much longer. Define how the application behaves then. It might display a freshness warning, omit a facility temporarily, or check each returned candidate against the source. Each choice changes latency, load or the user’s experience.</p>
   <p>Rechecking a candidate can remove a stale positive: search returns product 1, and the catalogue says it is inactive. It cannot discover a newly added product missing from the copy. Nor do separate reads from two services become an atomic snapshot because the application performs them one after another. A product can change again after the check. Rules that must hold when accepting an order belong in the authoritative purchase transaction.</p>
   <p>The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=copy-freshness">copy-freshness lesson</a> makes these states visible in two tables. You deliberately withhold an update, recheck a candidate and deliver changes out of order. Both tables are in one browser database: this models the values and update rule, without pretending to simulate a network, a replica or a second service.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="delivery"><h2>Outbox delivery and change capture</h2>
   <p>An application that writes the catalogue and then calls search has a gap. The catalogue commit may succeed and the process may stop before the search call. Reversing the calls only changes which incorrect partial outcome is possible. Retrying the request also raises the question of which side already applied it.</p>
   <p>One design records the catalogue change and a pending event in the same database transaction. A worker later reads those committed events and sends them to search. This is commonly called a <a href="https://microservices.io/patterns/data/transactional-outbox">transactional outbox</a>. It makes the local change and the obligation to publish it succeed together. It does not make the destination update part of that transaction.</p>
   <p>After search applies an event, the worker can stop before recording that it delivered it. On restart, it may send the event again. The destination therefore needs a safe repeated application. In our example, every committed catalogue change advances a per-product version, and the event carries the complete derived state for that version. The updater atomically accepts only a version newer than the stored one. The SQL model expresses that rule as:</p>
   <Sql code={apply} label="Apply a newer complete product state in the model" />
   <p><code>EXCLUDED</code> holds the proposed insert row; <code>choice_copy</code> holds the stored row it would replace. The comparison and replacement happen in one database operation. A separate “read the current version, then write” sequence would allow concurrent updaters to race. The real search product needs an equivalent supported conditional operation or a delivery arrangement that establishes the required ordering. Do not assume an API with an overwrite method provides that guarantee.</p>
   <p>This rule also depends on the event’s meaning. If version 3 contains the complete current title and active flag, it can supersede version 2 for this derived document. If events are increments such as “add one to a counter,” skipping one loses an effect; keeping only the largest version is insufficient. Define identities, ordering and duplicate handling for the actual operation.</p>
   <p>Withdrawal needs retained ordering information. Physically deleting the search record also deletes the version used to reject an old active event. A retained inactive record, or another durable record of the deletion version, prevents that replay from recreating the offer. How long this information must remain depends on the delivery and replay horizon. Deletion and retention requirements also apply to queues, logs and backups, as the <a href="/learn/databases/relational/data-lifecycle/">data-lifecycle guide</a> explains.</p>
   <section id="outbox"><h3>An interrupted delivery</h3>
   <p>We can follow the source obligation and the destination update through one interrupted delivery. The application sends a transaction on its catalogue connection. A separate worker later reads committed pending events on another source connection and calls the search service. Only after that service acknowledges the event does the worker record delivery back at the source. These are separate commits, with a possible interruption between them.</p>
   <figure class="outbox-sequence">
    <div class="outbox-stages">
     <div class="outbox-stage"><p class="panel-title">1 · Catalogue commit</p><p>Product 1 → version 2, inactive</p><p>Event 1/2 → pending</p><small>One local transaction</small></div>
     <span class="outbox-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
     <div class="outbox-stage"><p class="panel-title">2 · Search accepts</p><p>Store version 2, inactive</p><p>Event 1/2 remains pending at source</p><small>A separate destination operation</small></div>
     <span class="outbox-arrow" aria-hidden="true">→</span>
     <div class="outbox-stage"><p class="panel-title">3 · Record delivery</p><p>Worker receives acknowledgement</p><p>Mark source event 1/2 delivered</p><small>A later source transaction</small></div>
    </div>
    <figcaption>Stop after stage 2 and the source still has work to send. Repeating the immutable version-2 payload must be safe before the worker can acknowledge it at stage 3.</figcaption>
   </figure>
   <p>The <a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=outbox-delivery">outbox-delivery lesson</a> uses three new tables: <code>depth_catalogue</code>, <code>depth_outbox</code> and <code>depth_search</code>. Product 1 starts active at version 1 in catalogue and search; the outbox is empty. All three tables are in the browser's one database. You deliberately perform their changes in separate runs to inspect the states a real pair of systems could reach. No network failure is simulated.</p>
   <p>Each event contains the complete derived product state and is identified by the pair <code>(product_id, version)</code>. The application advances the version in the same UPDATE that withdraws the product. The returned row supplies the outbox payload:</p>
   <Sql code={outbox.steps[2].sql} label="Commit the product withdrawal and its pending event together" />
   <p><code>WITH changed</code> names the rows emitted by <code>UPDATE … RETURNING</code>. The INSERT consumes their new values; it does not reconstruct the event from an earlier application read. If no product is updated, no event is inserted, which the application must handle as a missing target. If inserting the event fails, the transaction cannot commit the product change alone. The lesson first runs this operation with ROLLBACK: the source remains active at version 1 and the outbox stays empty. The committed run leaves version 2 inactive and one pending event.</p>
   <p>If the application stops now, the worker can still discover the event with a query for <code>delivered_at IS NULL</code>. It reads the version-2 payload and sends it to search. For this complete-state protocol, the destination's conditional operation either installs a newer version or retains an equal or newer one. A successful response can acknowledge either result. The same event identity must always carry the same payload; reusing version 2 for a different product state would break that contract.</p>
   <p>In the lesson, search applies version 2 and becomes inactive, but we leave the event pending. That represents the worker stopping after the destination commit and before recording delivery. On restart the worker finds the same event. Its second attempt changes no destination row because version 2 is already present; the successful no-op is enough to finish this delivery. The worker then sets <code>delivered_at</code> for exactly product 1/version 2 on its source connection.</p>
   <p>A lost response differs from that successful no-op. Without a response, the worker leaves the event pending and retries within its operating policy. Marking it delivered <em>before</em> calling search would create a different failure: a stopped worker could leave no pending obligation even though search never received the withdrawal. Conversely, a failure after search accepts it can cause repeated delivery. Keeping the destination operation safe to repeat is what makes the latter case recoverable.</p>
   <p>The model has one sequential worker. A production queue also needs bounded batches, a way for concurrent workers to claim or lease work, recovery of abandoned claims, and visible handling of events that keep failing. A row lock held while a remote service stalls can retain a source connection and transaction for too long. Choose the worker protocol deliberately; a pending-events SELECT by itself does not coordinate a fleet of workers.</p>
   <p>Finally, the lesson delivers the old active version 1. Search retains inactive version 2, so the product stays withdrawn. This is the same ordering rule as before, now attached to a durable source obligation and an acknowledged delivery. The transport may repeat events. The product state converges because complete versions are applied conditionally, not because the network delivers each message exactly once.</p>
   </section>
   <h3 id="change-capture">Log-based change capture</h3>
   <p>Change capture from the database log is another way to obtain committed changes. It shifts the work toward interpreting records, retaining a recoverable stream position and mapping schema changes into the destination. It still needs an initial copy, replay rules and monitoring. The relevant choice is which delivery mechanism the team can operate correctly for these facts.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="rebuild"><h2>Rebuild and operate the copy</h2>
   <p>A derived store should have a credible route back from empty. That route becomes necessary after loss, corruption, a mapping change or a new search configuration. Copying the current catalogue while live changes continue introduces a race: an older backfill value can arrive after a newer event and overwrite it.</p>
   <p>A rebuild therefore needs a defined relationship between the source snapshot and the change stream. One approach, supported by facilities such as PostgreSQL’s <a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/logicaldecoding-explanation.html">exported logical-decoding snapshot</a>, establishes a consistent snapshot with a corresponding stream position, loads that snapshot into a new destination, then applies subsequent changes until caught up. Another uses a deliberately overlapping capture window with per-record versions that safely reconcile the overlap. Both require the source and capture mechanism to support the chosen boundary; a wall-clock timestamp written in a notebook is not enough.</p>
   <p>Before directing requests to the new destination, check representative records, missing/deleted products, version coverage and application queries. Record the latest source position it has applied and the lag under current writes. Cut over using a controlled routing or alias change with a return path that remains valid. Keeping an old index is only useful for rollback if its data and mapping still support the application.</p>
   <p>Measure the delivery process as part of the service. Queue depth alone cannot distinguish a healthy quiet shop from a stuck worker. Track the age of the oldest unapplied change, source and destination positions, failures, retries and a known recent test change reaching the searchable result. Decide who responds when freshness exceeds its allowed bound.</p>
   <p>Another store may isolate heavy reads, but it adds resource costs at the source too: event creation or capture, initial scans, retained history and catch-up traffic. During an outage, retained events can grow; after recovery, draining them can contend with live work. Rehearse that recovery load, rather than benchmarking only an already synchronised destination.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="decision"><h2>Decide from an observed limit</h2>
   <p>For the shop, keep search in PostgreSQL if its matching, ranking and measured resource use meet the requirement with room for expected traffic. Add a specialised search service if a demonstrated relevance or operating need justifies it and the team can maintain the copy’s contract. A feature list can suggest a candidate; representative queries and a recovery exercise establish whether it fits.</p>
   <p>For reporting, a separate system may give heavy analytical reads an appropriate layout and independent resources. State how current its figures are and which decisions may use them. A report that tolerates an hour of delay should not silently become the source for accepting a purchase that requires current stock.</p>
   <p>Write the decision around the observed limitation, the expected improvement and the new responsibilities: authority, freshness, delivery, rebuild, deletion and on-call ownership. Those are part of the design’s cost. They also tell a future engineer when the original choice deserves another look.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="series-reading"><h2>Further reading</h2><p><a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=search-in-postgres">Search words inside PostgreSQL →</a><span>Observe matching and a committed title change before investigating a separate service.</span></p><p><a href="/learn/databases/relational/sandbox/?tutorial=copy-freshness">Explore a stale copy and an old replay →</a><span>Use explicit versions to keep an older event from undoing a withdrawal.</span></p><p><a href="/learn/databases/relational/reporting/">Reporting alongside transactions →</a><span>Define the time and workload a report represents.</span></p></section>
 </ArticleBody>
